  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nPowerful upper low is currently working its way across the Desert \nSouthwest. What was left of Polo got quickly pulled northeast and is \nnow tracking across northeast NM.  At the same time a strong jet max \nis also lifting into eastern NM. \n\nA widespread area of showers and a few storms are lifting across the \nTX/Ok Panhandles in response to strong upper diffluence along \nwith very impressive 850-700mb moisture transport. The remnants \nof Polo are also aiding in the widespread rain to our \nsouthwest. This area of moderate to heavy rainfall will continue\nlifting northeast this morning and will encompass all of \nwestern and central KS by 12z. By the early afternoon, area of \nmoderate/heavy rain will be lifting into north central KS as \nsurface low develops over the OK Panhandle. Warm front will \nextend northeast from the surface low into north-central KS. \nThe main question for this afternoon will be if and where \nsurface based convection will develop. Most likely area would be\neast and northeast of the sfc low, which would generally affect\nour western flank, along and west of Highway 14. With very low \nLCLs and high 0-3km CAPE, it's not out of the question we see \nsome low topped supercells that could produce a couple brief \ntornadoes, like what happened over western KS Tuesday. With \ncloud bases so low, it won't take much to get any rotation to \nthe ground. \n\nUpper low will move out across the Southern High Plains tonight and \nby 12z Thu will be situated over far southwest KS. The main area for \nheavy rain tonight into Thu morning still looks to be from the \nFlint Hills into southeast KS. This is where the better 850mb \ntheta-e advection will be located along with right entrance \nregion of strong upper jet. With extreme PWs in place, any of \nthe stronger showers or storms tonight will likely produce very \nhigh rainfall rates and potential flooding. If the 12z model \ndata remains consistent, it's possible we may end up needing a \nflood watch for southeast KS for tonight. \n\nUpper low will quickly track east on Thu and also start to get \nsheared apart. As this occurs, precip will also shift \nsoutheast, with only se KS still seeing rainfall for Thu night. \nBy 12z Fri, what is left of this system will stretch from \ncentral MO and down through central/sw TX, with rain chances \nsoutheast of the forecast area. Precip is then expected to \nremain out of the forecast through the weekend with very \ncomfortable temps anticipated.",
